In my opinion, there are challenge conducts, that is, restricting your
actions voluntarily to make the game harder and thus more interesting for
an experienced player, and there are role playing conducts, that is,
restricting your actions voluntarily to fit a certain image of how a foo
should behave. These of course also make the game harder, but not
necessarily by much, and most players that follow role playing conducts
don't do it because they think NetHack is too easy, but because they like 
to play NetHack as an RPG. Anyone can do that when playing is more fun for
them that way.

My point was that there are many things in NetHack that don't fit a closed
RPG-like image (not as many as in Slash'EM, by far, but still a lot), and
that the common "solution" to this is to say that NetHack _isn't_ an RPG
per se, but if you want to play it as one, you can still do so without the
need to change anything in the game itself.

